    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0530f8e2-410e-586f-bc69-69d62dcdd47f",
      ":question/problem": "Find the particular solution to $\\sin(x - 1)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\left(8 x - 8\\right)\\sin(x - 1)=\\cos(x - 1)$ that satisfies $y(1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2})=\\pi^{2}$. Assume that $1<x<1 + \\pi$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We first rearrange and separate, then integrate and apply the initial condition.\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{\\cos(x - 1)}{\\sin(x - 1)}+8 x - 8.\n$$\n\nUse $v=\\sin(x - 1)$, so $\\mathrm{d}v=\\cos(x - 1)\\mathrm{d}x$. On the given interval, sine is positive. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int\\mathrm{d}y=\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{v}+\\int \\left(8 x - 8\\right)\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=\\ln(\\sin(x - 1))+4 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nAt $x=1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\sin(x - 1)=1$ and hence\n\n$$\n\\pi^{2}=\\pi^{2}+C,\\qquad C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $y=4 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\ln{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - 1 \\right)} \\right)}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
